:38,"confidence":0.87},"profileConfidence":0.78,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Alexandria","confidence":0.68}],"argument":{"statement":"Book collecting is a longstanding and widespread passion whose obsessive form constitutes a distinctive cultural phenomenon.","confidence":0.53}},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"e5438be2b852497fbb0646b3b04ae7826ee4168401136ece329dec4d00624ee9","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Society & Culture","secondarySubjects":["History"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book explores the cultural phenomenon of bibliomania and the obsession with book collecting over centuries, making Society & Culture the dominant subject while also incorporating elements of History.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"09363b810ce093b7b5dcf1615d7a1e16b034f90b37db4f79e0ef8d98838ad194","summary":"The passion to possess books has never been more widespread than it is today; indeed, obsessive book collecting remains the only hobby to have a disease named after it. A Gentle Madness is an adventure among the afflicted. Author Nicholas Basbanes, a dedicated bibliophile himself, begins his book 2,200 years ago in Alexandria, when a commitment was made to gather all the world's knowledge beneath one roof. In a series of lively chapters, the continuum then passes through the Middle Ages and the Renaissance to the twentieth century with a special emphasis on book lore and book culture in Great Britain and North America. In the second half of A Gentle Madness, Basbanes offers a gallery of revealing profiles of living collectors and presents exclusive examinations of the great contemporary stories. The book also includes the most comprehensive bibliography on book collecting compiled in more than a quarter century.","pageCount":638,"publicationYear":1995,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Biblio